<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinaloa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sinaloa is a state on the Pacific coast of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, with its capital at Culiacán. Under Spanish rule it was a frontier province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a> and evangelised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions from the 1590s. Its irrigated coastal plain later became one of Mexico's main farming regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sinaloa.jpg" alt="Sinaloa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Frausto Bernal · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sierra_Madre_Occidental....jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sinaloa is one of the 31 states that, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, make up the Federal Entities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It covers 58,328 square kilometres and is divided into 20 municipalities. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a>, Chihuahua, Durango and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayarit">Nayarit</a>, and faces Baja California Sur across the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-madre-occidental">Sierra Madre Occidental</a> dominates the east, and many rivers flow from it to the gulf through fertile valleys and a narrow coastal plain. The most popular explanation of the name, attributed to Eustaquio Buelna, combines the Cahita words sina, the pitahaya plant, and lobola, rounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sinaloa belongs to the northern limit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, and the Fuerte River marks the start of Aridoamerica to the north. Before European contact it was inhabited by groups such as the Cahitas, the Tahues, the Acaxees and the Xiximes. In 1531 Nuño Beltrán de Guzmán, with a force of over 10,000 men, established a Spanish and allied Indian outpost at San Miguel de Culiacán. Over the next decade the Cahíta suffered severe depopulation from conquest and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and other diseases brought by Europeans.</p><p>Starting in 1599 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries spread out from a base at what is now Sinaloa de Leyva, and by 1610 Spanish influence reached the northern edge of the territory. Their movement into the east prompted the Acaxee Rebellion of 1601; the Spanish executed 48 Acaxee leaders. After independence Sinaloa was joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a> as the Estado de Occidente, and in 1830 it separated to become the 20th state of the First Mexican Republic, with Culiacán as its capital. Los Mochis was founded in 1893 by American settlers led by Albert K. Owen as the Topolobampo colony, inspired by utopian socialism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital and largest city is Culiacán; other large cities include Mazatlán, a tourist resort, and Los Mochis. Irrigation projects built after the revolution turned arid land into farmland and earned Sinaloa the name granary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Banda music, popularised there in the late 19th century, is its best-known cultural tradition, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, a form of the ancient Mesoamerican ballgame, is still played in a few rural communities near Mazatlán.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sinaloa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The commandant general held military, political and judicial authority over Nueva Vizcaya, Sonora and <b>Sinaloa</b>, the Californias, New Mexico, Coahuila and Texas, and at times Nuevo León and Nuevo Santander.</blockquote><a href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</a></li></ul></section>
